Ewald Peters <ewald_peters@yahoo.com> wrote:
> Hello Vax-Fans!

> I tried to install NetBSD 1.4.1 since 1 year without
> success.

Sorry about that, it should install much easier than that.

> Does anybody have some experience with this topic?
> I managed to netboot the VaxStation from a Linux-box
> but after loading the kernel the thing hangs.
> I read that the bootmessages are sent to the
> serial output, but i never got a connection.
> What Pinouts? How to connect? What Plug?
> I tried a few things. Nothing worked.

I have 4 VAXstations up with NetBSD.  It works great.

The console serial is on the printer port after you switch the
little hidden console mode switch next to the idiot light panel
on the back.  Switch it up to use the printer port as your console,
down to use the normal monitor/keyboard as the console.

I use a VT420 on mine as console, most of the time, via a straight
MMJ cable.  Anything running some sort of vt100 mode will work.
PCs or normal terminals will require a null connection.  DEC terminals
wire straight through.

> With some kernels (i experimented a little)
> pinging the mashine works, but no telnet service.

Hmm, strange.  Telnet should work fine out either of the ethernet
ports.  Check which port is active by looking for a little green
idiot light net to either the bnc connector or the aui connector.
There is a switch in the middle that you can press once to go one
way and again to go the other way.  I use bnc nets, myself, since
it is cheaper, shielded, and does not require a hub.

> With the normal NetBSD 1.4.1 netboot-kernel
> (i renamed the original Filesystem from boot.fs to netbsd)
> pinging the VaxStation does not work after
> the 'kernel boot hanging'
> Any ideas?

Boot.fs should not be renamed netbsd.  The netbsd kernel is installed
out of kern.tgz during the installation process.  The boot.fs is a
speparate install system that should really be in swap or on floppy
for booting, or maybe on tk50 tape.  Check the INSTALL doc for details.

As an alternative, if you have a VAX Ultrix that you can load, you
can copy the boot.fs into swap on a minimal Ultrix install and then
boot/install NetBSD from that.

You might try subscribing to the netbsd newsfeed or to the port-vax
mailinglist at netbsd.org, for more details.

VAXen be funztoyz fer boyz, and people are almost throwing them away
these days.  VAXstations are good for NetBSD play.  I would recommend
after you get it going to move to a current kernel and snapshot build,
since new high-speed scsi drivers are in the current builds.  NetBSD-1.4P
seems to be the most recent snapshot.  To load it, cp the netbsd-1.4P
kernel to root, mv the old netbsd-1.4.1 kernel to a spare copy (e.g.,
netbsd.141) and cp the new kernel to netbsd.  Reboot.  Unroll the
build 1.4P tarballs from root as tar -xpzf /wherever/base.tgz,
then etc, then all the rest of the required tarballs.  Reboot, and
you are loaded into the current system.

Good LUck.

Bob




