>  Brian Tillman                   Internet: tillman_brian at si.com
>  Smiths Industries, Inc.                   tillman at swdev.si.com
>  3290 Patterson Ave. SE, MS      Addresses modified to prevent
>  Grand Rapids, MI 49512-1991     SPAM.  Replace "at" with "@"
>         This opinion doesn't represent that of my company

After a one year trial with our last VAX system, we switched all of our
stuff over to SMS just over a year ago. We've been pretty happy with them
since. I'd say the only drawbacks are (a) they aren't everywhere so they
can't support one of our offices and (b) they don't support leading edge
hardware, so systems like a GS140 and ES40 are still on DECpaQ contract.

A couple things we do like about SMS is that they include a few hours of
extra support per month. We've used these credits for new installs,
reconfiguring hardware, etc. Another feature is that rather than stocking
spare parts in a warehouse, they have systems up and running in a data
center. They get parts from these systems. So you know that until you logged
the call, the hardware was up and running. This has decreased the incidence
of DOA parts. Plus most parts, like an HSJ controller they can configure to
match our failed unit before they arrive at our site. This reduced downtime.

With any maintenance vendor, 90% of the satisfaction is dependent on PEOPLE.
Most of the SMS folks we deal with are ex-DECcies, amny of them folks I knew
as a DECcie myself. I'm sure this is true of many other vendors. Talk with
other sites IN YOUR AREA covered by any proposed third party vendor. They
will tell you if the service is good or not.

	Bob Kaplow	

