Case 1)  You still want to share drives between the the uVAX systems

You'd be looking for either an HSD05, HSD10, HSD30, or HSD50.  You connect
the SCSI drives to either of these HSDx controllers which in turn is
connected to the DSSI bus.

The HSD50 supports six SCSI channels with six devices each.  The HSD30
supports three SCSI channels with six devices each.  The HSD10 supports a
single SCSI channel with six devices, as does the HSD05.  If memory serves
me right, the HSD05 could only handle an aggregate bandwidth of 1MB/s
whereas the other three controllers were rated at 3MB/s.

Both the HSD05 and HSD10 require a StorageWorks cabinet (BA350-SB or
BA350-Kx) which holds the HSD controller and drives.  The HSD30 and HSD50
mount in the controller slot of a BA350-MB cabinet, then you attach other
cabinets (again, BA350-SB or BA350-Kx) containing the drives themselves.  Or
you can use a SW300 cabinet which houses the HSD30/50 controller and up to
12/24 drives.  (In the SW300 you'd be limiting each SCSI channel of the
controller to only four drives each. Therefore, the HSD30 could support 12
drives in that cabinet, and the HSD50 can support 24 drives.)

Having recently studied this stuff to move a client from DSSI drives to
SCSI, a lot of it is still fresh in my mind.

Case 2)  Shared devices not necessary

Find a CMD CQD-220 (I believe) Q-Bus SCSI controller for each system.  This
controller supports six directly-connected SCSI devices.  They are mounted
externally in the cabinet of your choosing (or standalone, if that's the way
you like them).

Hope that helps.


