In article <38B9F1DE.7BDCDEA1@bomarcspam.com>, Dan French <dan@bomarc.com> writes:
> I'm trying to locate a ALPHA 275 / 300 mhz (working) CPU, and/or
> information about the CPU.  Compaq was less then insightful in providing
> information.

You don't specify the generation. The only 275 MHz systems were EV4/275,
found in the 2X00 series. The only 300 MHZ were EV5/300, in several system
types. These are hardly interchangable, at either the chip or board level.
They are MUCH more different than 275 vs 300, it's more like a 66%
difference. What are you really looking for, a chip, or a board, and for
what box?
                                                           
> Q1: Does anyone know of a source where these can be optioned?
> 
> Q2: Can a "faster" Alpha cpu be "under clocked" (instead of "over
> clocked") and still work? If so, what speed(s) of CPU can be "under
> clocked"?  (For example, I know that I can put a P166 into a P133
> system, and get P133 speeds out of it.  Can I so something similar with
> the Alpha CPU?)

The chip itself can most likely be over or under clocked. DEC didn't make
the chips differently (yes, there are different generations of alpha that
are different), the manufacturing process naturally yields some chips that
work faster than others. Those that run at 250 get sold as 250s, those that
run at 300 get sold as 300s, those that run 625 get sold as 625s. Intel,
Motorola, and others do the same.

