Alexander R Svirsky wrote:
> 
> I've got a TK50 tape drive that gives me the rapid red flashing light
> drive fault error.  I have checked the leader tab to see that it is
> correctly placed in its hook.
> 
> This drive has not been used for a few years and has been in storage.  I
> have periodically plugged it in and powered it up to see that it always
> did pass the self test and give me the green ready light.  It was last
> powered up about 6-8 months ago.  When I needed it to restore tapes to a
> DS5000/200 it gave me the blinking red fault on power-up.  Sigh.
> 
> I've checked the tape leader tab and it looks good.  There are no other
> obvious visible mechanical faults.  The only thing I can think of is that
> lubricants may have dried due to disuse.

There are two possibilities:

1.  Dust in the opto-interrupters on the TK50 drive.  There are
two LED emitter/sensor pairs, of which one pair is in fairly plain
sight after you remove the metal cover from the drive assembly.  This
pair is fairly easy to dust off with compressed air.

The other pair is in the "takeup" side capstan.  If you take the
metal cover off the drive assembly, you'll see two shiny metal
capstans.  The one on the rear has a LED emitter/sensor pair to
sense capstan motion.  Getting at this pair is more difficult, but
worth a try.

2.  The other common cause of TK50's malfunctioning is that the
takeup capstan isn't turning as tape (or leader) is pulled across
it, and the reason it isn't turning is usually becuase the
bearings in the capstan aren't as good as they were when new.
The TK50 electronics likes to see that capstan turning as tape
moves, and if it doesn't see that capstan turning it'll give up
rather than (potentially) damage the tape.

