Product lifecycles and option availability tends to be a case-by-case thing.
On the one hand, people always want faster/cheaper products, so you want to
get the newest, fastest, cheapest stuff out to replace the existing
hardware.  The vast majority of customers buy a system, and never really
change anything on it for a long time (except when something fails).

When a box has been popular, you will find that new options will be
qualified for it, because there is still a large demand.  As time goes on,
the demand to qualify new options on old hardware diminishes... and so
qualification of HW on older products becomes a product-specific decision.

In the case of the DS20, it was very popular, and there have been a number
of variations of the box.  In asking around (and up until a short while ago,
I was the Technical Director of the OpenVMS Systems Group) I can't find
anyone here saying that the DS20 is going to eol "soon"... but because of
its polularity, it may be one of the first to be replaced with the next
generation of core chipsets and processors.  When that happens, you'll find
something that looks pretty much like the DS20, but has faster CPUs, and
additional features.



