HerbalGypsy/justbobthebard (shsrms@bellatlantic.net) wrote:
: Michael,
: be careful with PS.
: The 11/45 is a +5 hog.

Yes, there are multiple 5V 25A power modules in the machine... The 
official PSU is a pair of H742 units, the 'top' one being switched by the 
panel key and being used to run the CPU boards (IIRC, it's 3 or 4 5V 
modules and a -15V one), while the lower PSU runs all the time and 
supplies the semiconductor memory. Which modules go in here (and there 
are some really odd ones) depends on what memory you have.

: the orig supply was a set of switchers and finicky.

Hving worked on one, I find these old DEC power modules to be a lot 
easier to repair than modern SMPSUs. At least there are no lethal 
voltages on the switching regulator boards -- they run off 30V AC 
windings on the transformer.

: > 1) The "easiest": the machine lacked a power supply.  Being a budding EE,
: >    I've collected a bunch of power supplies, and am designing a new supply
: >    for it.  Correct me if I'm wrong, but I think I need +-5V, +-15V, +20V,

You need multiple 5V lines (IIRC the CPU + MMU uses 2 off 5V 25A 
regulators, the floating point boards use another 5V 25A unit, and there 
are more for the Unibus peripheral backplanes). You need +15V at about 4A 
(not much uses this -- RS232 buffers and panel lights), maybe 8V at a 
minimal current, -15V @ 10A (RS232 buffers, clock generator, etc), plus 
whatever your memory needs. Core tended to need +20V and -5V IIRC.

Do you have the CPU printset (schematics). You _are_ going to need it.
 
: >    and a few AC lines (to run the fans).  Anyone know roughly how much

The fans, at least in a 'stock' 11/45 are 115V mains units wired in 
parallel. For US mains, they're simply connected to the mains. For 
European 230V mains, they run off the primary of the main power 
transformer, using the centre tap as an autotransformer.

I have the CPU prints for this machine, and I also have a working 11/45 
here. So I can look things up if you like.

-tony




