Michael Robinson (robinm@rpi.edu) wrote:
: On the continuing PDP-11/45 Power Supply saga...

: Tony Duell (ard@p850ug1.demon.co.uk) wrote:
: : The
: : official PSU is a pair of H742 units, the 'top' one being switched by the 
: : panel key and being used to run the CPU boards 

: Actually, I just traced the lines to the keyswitch this afternoon.  I think

The keyswitch doesn't actually connect to the PSU AFAIK. It connects to 
the power controller (860 or 861 depending on the age of the machine), 
which is basically a big relay box to turn on the mains to the PSUs.

: (since I have core, and not semiconductor memory; and more importantly,
: no key) that I'll just use an external switch for the whole works.

: : You need multiple 5V lines (IIRC the CPU + MMU uses 2 off 5V 25A 
: : regulators, the floating point boards use another 5V 25A unit, and there 
: : are more for the Unibus peripheral backplanes). You need +15V at about 4A 
: : (not much uses this -- RS232 buffers and panel lights), maybe 8V at a 
: : minimal current, -15V @ 10A (RS232 buffers, clock generator, etc), plus 
: : whatever your memory needs. Core tended to need +20V and -5V IIRC.
:  
: OK, having spent the last month tracing basically *every* wire in the
: power cable to its location into the backplane, or wherever, the voltages
: on several lines is no surprise.  The currents are in line with what
: I'd guess, except the -15V, which is a bit  higher than I guessed.

It may well not need anything like that much -- the DEC regulator can 
supply 10A, though, but it's a standard unit used in other machines. 
 
: How much do the RS232 buffers take, seeing as we may not even have the
: appropriate boards?  (Is M7800 an RS232-esque board?  If so, ours seems
: to have a broken chip (ie. broken in half...))

The M7800 is the DL11 console interface, and it exists in RS232/current 
loop and current loop only versions. If you have 1488 amd 1489 chips on 
the board, then it's the RS232 version (if not, you can add them).

Prints for the M7800 exist, so it should be possible to find out what 
chip you need and replace it (AFAIK there's nothing remotely custom 
on that board).

: : Do you have the CPU printset (schematics). You _are_ going to need it.

: Uuh, no.  (That's why I spent last month tracing wires...) 

You need them. Period. Seriously, the 11/45 was my first PDP11, and I 
learnt a lot from repairing it and getting it to work again. But I 
wouldn't even attempt to work on one without the printset, unless you're 
some kind of deity :-). 

The 11/45 CPU is _complex_, and it's got all sorts of optimisations in 
the hardware/microcode that are not obvious from looking at the machine, 
and which will cause you endless fun when trying to debug a logic fault. 
That is the voice of experience...

-tony




