Not only that, gcc will have a real hard time generating code for the
Fortran programs which are dominate in most parts of the technical market.
I would not use C compiler results for evaluating the success of a product
in the technical market.  :-)

[Yes, C is used in many technical market applications, and is even the only
language for some, but for most, the execution time is dominated by Fortran
code.]

Whatever the current code quality of ia64 compilers, lots of changes can
occur between now and volume shipments of product.  My past experience with
new compilers on new architectures shows that there are frequently dramatic
gains in short periods of time, followed by the usual slower rate of
improvements after the easy things are done.

A short time ago, there were no real hardware systems which ran the IA64
instruction set.  A short time in the future, there will be a reasonable
number  of development systems available for doing compiler tuning.  I would
not try to judge how fast the IA64 architecture can be before compiler
tuners have had six months to a year on real hardware.

Simulators are all very well, but you can run more real code in the first
month of real hardware than in all the development time before then.

- Patrick McGehearty



