Thanks you for your reply.  My discovery about the status of the DS20 came
about because, since the time of my purchase, I have wanted to upgrade the
"modular" UltraSCSI disk Drive cards that were initially certified for the
machine.  At the time I bought it, I pointed out to my vendor and people at
DEC that there was an inconsistency between the high tech mother board
hardware and the one-generation-out-of-date UltraSCSI drive controllers
available for the machine.  The Ultra-II-SCSI cards have been available for
at least the past 9 months and were already available on competitor
machines, as well as the Compaq Proliant server line at the time I purchased
the DS20.  THerefore I was led to believe they would be available for the
DS-20 soon.  My work is all database related and in spite of my 512MB system
with dual processor, my operational bottleneck is the speed with which the
data is read from the drive.

I have been calling at intervals to check the availability of the
Ultra-II-SCSI cards for the DS-20, and after a few iterations of "Not Yet"
the answer changed to "Not EVER!"

I stand by the "end-of-life" status of the DS-20, having had many
discussions in the past two weeks with Compaq on the subject.  (I recognize
by your e-mail address that you are affiliated with DEC.  I am curious about
which division??)  I have been informed that I will be able to add memory,
or if something breaks it will be replaced, but, as I said, even a
straightforward upgrade that you can do yourself in a cheap WinTel product
will not be possible in the DS-20.  All the upgrades at this level of the
Alpha family are being focused on the DS-20E.  The customer service
representative at Compaq keeps reminding me that this is what happens with
technology advances.  I disagree since there is no technological reason that
I can think of that an would prevent an Ultra II SCSI card  from being
developed and certified for the DS-20 -- this sounds more like a business
decision to simplify the product line.  This sort of thing seems to happen a
lot with PC's, I just didn't think it happened so quickly with higher end
servers.

My insight, and what I had been led to believe by Compaq personel agrees
with you contention that "In addition, the suite of systems from the DS10,
DS20, ES40, and even the XP1000 all use a common basic design in terms of
the core chipset -- and are all PCI based, so options from one, typically
work without change in another. "  The information I have been receiving
from Compaq is that this cross-platform compatibility does not exist.

Any further insights would be helpful!

Mark



