Dirk Kleinhesselink <dkleinh@phy.ucsf.edu> wrote:
: I found a couple of DEC PCI SCSI/ethernet combo cards in the back of our
: computer room and I wondered if anyone could tell me what they are:  The
: cards have 2 internal 68 pin SCSI connectors and no external SCSI
: connector.  They have BNC AUI and RJ-45 ethernet connector on the end.
: The cards are very long and have a handle on the inside end of the
: card.  The boards have 2 Qlogic ISP1020 chips on them, a DEC chip 21050
: and a DEC 21040-AA chip and a large chip that says HALO TNT on them.
: The cards have 3 jumpers on them, 1 jumper says DIFF with ENA DIS
: underneath, identified by W6; the other 4 jumpers seem to be in pairs,
: W2 = LOW (ENA DIS) W1 = HIGH (ENA DIS) and W3 similar to W1 and W4
: similar to W2.  The cards have a bar code sticker: KA532CEUPH.  Anyone
: know what these cards are (I presume dual Qlogic 1020 but are they
: differential or SE ?  What are the jumper meanings ?

: Thanks,
: Dirk

Sounds like the SCSI/Ethernet combination controller used on AlphaStation
600 5/266 & later models.  The external SCSI for this card was handled by
(2) connectors which mounted to the workstation itself, kind of like those
serial & parallel connector mount points on the back of a PeeCee case that
rarely get used.  DEC used to offer an internal-to-external adapter that
took up ane expansion slot as well, from what I recall.  I think these may
have been used on some of the earlier AlphaServer 1000 models as well. 
FWIW, you can get internal-to-external cable adapters several places,
www.dalco.com is one I can think of off the top of my head...

A later poster identified this as a KZPSM, but I can't recall if that's the
same P/N as the 5/266 used or not.  They're twin-channel Qlogic 1020s, one
channel drove an external Narrow connector, the other ran all the internal
peripherals and an external Wide connector.  It's a single-ended card, with
10BaseT ethernet.

The DIFF jumper *MIGHT* enable differential SCSI mode, but I wouldn't bank
on it...You might be able to find out more on qlogic's website.  If I had to
make a guess, the W1/W2/W3/W4 jumper settings control the presentation or
abscence of high-bit SCSI ID's on the (2) channels (i.e. whether SCSI ID's
above 7 are usable).  There were issues with accessing ID's above 7 in some
of the earlier revs of Digital UNIX, IIRC, and in the DOS/Windows world as
well...

Chris

