Compiler performance is going to be critical to the success of
the IA64 line.  Even then, the statements in the media would
suggest that the underlying implementation will not be optimal,
saying that the real performance will not be apparent until
Merceds successor has been released.

As for power consumption and die size for Alpha, see:

    http://www.europe.digital.com/alphaoem/present/sld020.htm

With a huge onchip L2 cache, the 21364 will run 100 watts.
On the other hand, the predicted 70/120 SPECint/fp95
numbers are going to be extremely hard to beat, and the
level of integration on chip should make it easy to build
multiprocessor systems.

What the Alpha really needs is more sales and marketing.
Im glad to see some Alphas finally listed under the
"workstation" catagory of Compaqs web pages (for a while
they werent), but its going to take more than simply
acknowledging that you sell a product to get mainstream
acceptance for it.

