Early last month, my electronics club found a PDP-11/45 being disposed of
(actually in a trash heap!).  We decided to give it another chance at life,
and brought it (and several UNIBUS cards that were separate from the machine)
into our lab.  We have three major problems, one probably almost solved.

1) The "easiest": the machine lacked a power supply.  Being a budding EE,
   I've collected a bunch of power supplies, and am designing a new supply
   for it.  Correct me if I'm wrong, but I think I need +-5V, +-15V, +20V,
   and a few AC lines (to run the fans).  Anyone know roughly how much
   current to expect on each of these lines?

2) The most critical issue:  Apparently, this machine was "roughed up"
   before we got it.  Fortunately, only one board is unusable.  Unfortunately,
   we don't have a replacement.  It is a M8108 (apparently a memory management
   board).  We *really* need this board (unless I'm mistaken, we can't run 
   the machine without it).  Any ideas?

3) Finally, this machine was equipped with core memory.  When we found the
   machine, the memory and controller boards were out of the case, and
   hidden in a cardboard box.  This could mean that the memory was not for
   this machine.  Any ideas on the proper ordering of the boards?  Here's
   what appears to be part of our memory: M8293, G114, H217C (the core
   board, I have heard rumors that there is a second one of these floating
   around), and a G235.

Well, thanks in advance for any help you can provide.  Frankly, I could
