Microvaxen aren't real bad in that area, my Microvax II lives happily in the
back of my workshop.

> if I get it working, I would like it to do more than just oscillate.
> I've got a LAN at home, and would like to get the system to talk with
> the other computers....
>    Is that possible?  (With in the realm of sanity, and trying...)

Yes, it can run NetBSD Unix if you fancy it, or it may well still have
VMS probably 5.x, on it already.

> Anyway, the one thing that I did NOT get was any blank 9 track tapes, or
> disks with any form of OS on them.  I've found Lunix for the MicroVax,
> but I don't know if I can get it loaded... (transfer of the data to a
> form that the VAX can use)

It's possible, but very tedious, to install BSD from TK50, but over the
network
is better and faster.  The BSD web site for the Vax Port goes into this.

>    Also -- the issue of network card for it.  It appears to use "thick
> net", (on the back) and I don't think that I've got the hardware to
> transfer to thin-net.

They would have a 15 pin AUI so you just need a transciever of some sort to
translate to 10Base2 or 10BaseT to match your personal lan's topology.

There is a free (hobbyist) license for VMS that covers most of the
apps and layered products, including TCPIP, (there are several stacks
available for VMS apart from the DEC variant).  NetBSD, naturally, will
speak TCPIP natively.

> The nine track tape units are **VERY HEAVY**.  I'm still trying to get
> the second one out of my car --  tweaked my back moving the first.

Model Designations?  Probably MSCP (STI) drives. But they might
be Pertec or (if your're very lucky) SCSI.

> At this time I am still VERY early in the discovery of this system.
> Advice -- and/or pointers would be appreciated...

The Microvax FAQ contains much useful info.

Take a look at http://anacin.nsc.vcu.edu/~jim/mvax/mvax_faq_text.html

The OpenVMS FAQ is also a good source, and contains some hardware stuff as
well, it
has specific references to certain Microvax hardware and is worth a look.
The VMS FAQ is at (amongst other places) http://eisner.decus.org/vms/faq.htm

The DEC Module Database at http://www.neurotica.com/dec-info/ may help you
to identify
the various modules in the system(s).

Planet Vax at http://world.std.com/~bdc/projects/vaxen/ has a lot of stuff
with particular reference
to NetBSD on Vax/Microvax systems.

comp.os.vms is good for specifics on the VMS operating system, (but PLEASE
read the FAQ first)

There is a mailing list for the Vax NetBSD port accessible from the
netbsd.org web site.  Most older
Vaxen can run it in some form or another.

There is a DEC proprietary version of Unix called ULTRIX that ran on Vaxen,
but it's rare and difficult
to get (lawfully) for a number of reasons, and was somewhat bug-ridden, (or
so I'm told).

Best of luck,

Cheers

