>  1280 x 1024 at 66Hz, and 1280 x 1024 at 72Hz.

My experience is that these switches do absolutely nothing at all.  The
-DA variants are 66Hz fixed-frequency and will *not* handle 72Hz, unlike
the -HA models which auto-sense between the two.  (Sony GDM-1961)

>:The VLC4000 is a VS48K-CA.
>
>  There are a couple of different graphics controllers used with the
>  VAXstation 4000 VLC -- knowing what the console calls the graphics
>  display would be handy.  Use the alternate console to find out...

If no-one has done a board-swap, that is the part number of the base
unit supplied with the hi-res graphics option.  1280x1024@72Hz.

>:The video output on the VLC seems identical to the 4000/60, the usual 3 wire
>:RGB BNC cable.

If this is the "3 mini-coax in a d-shell" connector (like Sun's 13W3 but
without the pins), then I believe it.  The standard hi-res controller in
the 4000/60 is sadly only a 66Hz board.  So, same family, same
connectors, different refresh rates.  (Odd that the cheaper VLC had the
higher spec graphics, but then the /60 was offered with 24-bit and
multi-head cards if you really wanted performance.)

>:While we're at it, the VLC only has one of the old-fashined ethernet
>:adapters, one of those big plugs. I'm sorry I don't know the proper type
>:description.  I do have RJ45 and BNC ethernet in the area.  What is the
>:simplest way to get the VLC hooked up to the network?
>
>  This is AUI connection originally intended to connect to a "ThickWire" 
>  (10Base5) Ethernet transceiver such as the H4000 or H4005.  You will 
>  need a transceiver such as a DESTA or DECXM, which will permit the AUI 
>  transceiver cable to connect to a ThinWire (10Base2) network.  Single- 
>  or multiport repeaters are typically used to interconnect twisted pair 
>  (10BaseT) to ThinWire networks.

Assuming Henk has a drop cable...  The cheapest and easiest answer is a
mini AUI-BNC or AUI-RJ45 transceiver which plugs straight in the back
and allows direction connection to the thin-wire or TP network.
Available from Allied Telesys, D-Link, etc, for about $20-30 (in the
UK).

