Problem solved.  Thanks to GlennB who sent me:

Hi,

As I recall from many years ago, this is a particular piece of sabotage from

Digital.  They implemented a different ECC scheme on DEC badged SCSI disks
(with re-blown microcode) and then perform an enquiry on power up to see if
the drive supports it.  i.e. Are you Digital or not?  If not, then Error.

In some cases the boot sequence continues as normal and the disk is
completely useable - with its own ECC protection intact.  However most
sysadmins get scared when they see the error and who can blame them.  When I

was a DEC engineer the story was that no errors were acceptable, and that if

error like this occurred then who knows what else might happen?

In the 3rd party sector the suppliers learnt to re-code their
controllers/disks to bypass the error.  Seagate already have enough on their

plate with their own microcode so I don't see them changing this themselves.

This is not posted publicly as I might be wrong in your case.  However, I
would like to know the outcome.

Glenn B

I just proceeded with the boot and ignored the error.  Everything came up
fine.

Scott

