In comp.sys.dec Thomas Pornin <pornin@bolet.ens.fr> wrote:
> According to Zane H. Healy <healyzh@shell1.aracnet.com>:
>> I shudder to think what the disk space requirements for NetBSD/VAX
>> might be. I was horrified to learn I couldn't fit NetBSD V1.4.1 on a
>> 500MB HD on my DECstation 5000/133 (I got the system specifically for
>> NetBSD).

Yeah, it takes about 1 gig to unroll sources and have any leftover space.
MIPS machines tend to be space hogs.  VAXen are nice and tidy by comparison.

> This should be NetBSD/pmax, not NetBSD/vax: the 5000/133 is a mips,
> not a vax. What you tell is surprising: I just checked, the whole
> NetBSD install (including X11) takes about 160MB. This deserves
> investigation.

The blank install of everything less sources runs well in a 400mb
drive (RZ25ish) (on VAXen or MIPS).  But more is needed if you do
any compiling.  Watch out for swap space on the 5000/xxx machines,
as it will default to 3 x ram which on my machines runs about 330
mb swap.  Plan for that or use ram accordingly.  On a DS5000/25 toy
you can install on one RZ24 but it is very tight.  A MV3100 fits
with lots of space on the same drive.

Good Luck.

Bob



