Liz,

The DECpc XL 590 has an older Intel Neptune NX chipset which supports
only fast page mode memory, not the newer EDO.  Maximum amount of
memory is 192MB, consisting of 6 32MB SIMMs, if you can find fast page
mode in the 32MB size.  Adding memory is easy.

If the main logic board (the big board on the bottom of the case) in
your system is DEC part number 54-23606 and the processor card (stands
perpendicular to the main logic board, held in by a long metal bracket
and screw) is 54-24490, then you can go to 200MHz in a single
processor, 166MHz in a dual, and 233MHz MMX with a PowerLeap adapter. 

If you don't have 54-24490, then the max is 133MHz, dual processor.

If the main logic board is earlier than 54-23606 (lower numbered),
then only a single processor is supported, max is 133MHz.

The only tricky part of a processor upgrade is that the processor
sockets do not have enough clearance to handle the clips that usually
hold on a heat sink or a cooling fan.  So DEC CPUs have permanently
bonded heat sinks.

If the processor card is earlier than 54-24490, replacing it is an
easy operation.  If the main logic board is earlier than 54-23606, I
don't recommend replacing it if you don't know your way around
computers very well.  It is a tedious operation, more than difficult.

The on-board SCSI host adapter (NCR, now Symbios, chipset) supports
narrow non-ultra SCSI or SCSI-2 drives and other SCSI devices.  And
they don't have to carry the DEC brand name.

Why would you want to do any of this?  The DECpc XL and Celebris XL
systems are ruggedly built.  It's a darn shame that a seller of junk
like Compaq bought out DEC.  No appreciation for quality in design and
engineering.  Lots of culture clashes.

Cost effectiveness of any upgrades depends on how expensive the parts
are.  Some of them are extremely inexpensive nowadays.  If you need
any DEC-specific parts such as DEC processors, processor cards, etc.,
let me know.  I have a few left at pretty attractive prices.

... Ben Myers

