Hello all,
   Myself and my electronics club at RPI recently acquired a VAX 8530 system
that was being tossed by our computing department.  After much todo, we
finally got the three-phase power necessary to run it wired into our
lab.  So, we turned on the juice and here's a brief account of what 
happened.

1) Set things up:  Hooked the CPU cabinet up to the power.  Switched off the
   breaker in the back of the machine (right near the plug), and switched off
   the breaker under the lower front panel.  We attached a serial terminal to
   the console plug (running 9600-8-N-1).
2) Turned on the power to the outlet (we had the three-phase breaker off
   entirely.  We were immediately greeted by three lights glowing above the
   plug.  This is contrary to what the installation manual we have says.
3) We then switched on the breaker in the back of the machine.  At this
   point, nothing seemed to change, though the manual says that at this point
   the lights come on.  Further, the blowers should come on too.
4) Undaunted, we then turned on the breaker under the lower front panel, and
   after a few moments saw a zero appear on the little LED display.  The
   manual says that this is good.
5) Nothing we could do seemed to get us past this point.

We couldn't seem to get the blowers (or anything else, for that matter) 
running.  The serial console displayed nothing.  I personally suspect that
we need a real console.  Is there a way to get a PC to emulate such a device
(in the manual it looks like a MicroVAX or MicroPDP sort of machine)?
Perhaps something (hopefully simple) we need to do?  In any event, the machine
was in working condition when we got it...

Thanks in advance,
Michael Robinson
