in article 0nif88.93i.ln@localhost, Stuart R. Fuller at
stu@c49395-a.wodhvn1.mi.home.com wrote on 2/16/00 9:10 PM:

> Do you have a monitor connected to the video card?  Some of the video tests
> actually check to see if there's a monitor connected, and will fail if one is
> not connected.
> 
> Failing that, try reseating the video card (the PMAGB-BA is the video card in
> slot 0).  Failing that, you have a broken video card.
> 
> Stu
> 
> Chris H (newsgroupNOJUNK@NOJUNKhome.com) wrote:
> : They are :
> : ?TFL : #0 PMAGB-BA LINE : 15 : Line Signature Mode Test
> : ?TFL : #0 PMAGB-BA PATT: 5 : Red screen Test
> : 
> : what are these means ?

    While I'm no expert, I would guess that either a.) he has some bad VRAM
b.) the red video connector is not making proper contact or the cable is
damaged. c.) the monitor is not doing what it's supposed to d.) all.
    My PMAG-C responds ?TFL : PMAG-C : vdac +4+ (cabl) or something to that
effect when no monitor is attached. I think the first thing to check is to
check the resistance between the card and the end of the cable.
Unfortunately, this is a rather difficult thing to do because the card
connections are shielded. So you would have to measure from the underside of
the card to the center of the BNC cable. It should be between 50-80ohms.
IIRC, bad VRAM can also cause the above errors, but VRAM isn't usually
intermittently bad. Do you notice and lines, dots or strange colors when it
does it's screen test (the full screen r,g and b boxes)? That is usually a
tell tail sign of bad VRAM as well.
    Can you post the output of cnfg 0? You're using a VRC-16 right?
If nothing else maybe you could ship it to me or someone nearby and see if
we get the same results with known good hardware.
    

    Chris




