> to give on the matter.  I'd be very grateful.  Thanks,

I don't think FreeBSD will run on a DEC3000-M600.
Allthough the Alpha port of FreeBSD is getting very good, I think it's
only "good" with PCI (and maybe EISA/ISA) based Alphas, and not
TurboChannel Alphas.
The same goes for Alpha Linux.

I've run NetBSD on a DEC3000-M600. There were one minor bug in the
install procedure back then, but I got it running and it worked fine
(allthough I didn't have a frame buffer on the machine, so I didn't test
X except XDMCP from another machine).
I don't quite remember, but I think I also had OpenBSD running on a M600
about a year ago.

I think the best options are NetBSD or Tru64 (Digital Unix). You can get
the latest Tru64 as a "private" license for $99.
Right now I'd probably prefer to run dunix 4.0f on a 3000, but it will
run 5.0 without any problems if you prefer the latest (be advised that
5.0 still has a few bugs in it... it's better to wait for 5.0a).

I'm currently running 4.0f on several M600s at work. Runs very well,
even with limited memory (I have 64 MB).

Regarding frame buffers, you need TurboChannel adapters. There are a few
models, and they're all named something like this : PMAGB-BA
You need a special cable and a fixed-frequency monitor with
sync-on-green to get any usable out of a PMAG adapter.
If I remember it correctly, you can use the same TC adapters for both
DECstation 5000/25 & TC based Alphas.

None of the M600s I use have frame buffers, so I don't have much
experience in this are... but I've got a DECstation 5000/25 at home with
a 8 bit color framebuffer card running at 1280x1024 on a 19" monitor.
Works well, allthough very slow.... much of the slowness is probably due
to the pathetic CPU on the DEC5000/25 (a MIPS R3000 running at 25 MHz,
while the DEC3000-M600 has a EV4 Alpha running at 175 MHz).

Regarding hard drives, they're usually born with Quantum drives... but
they'll accept just about any normal single-ended SCSI disk.
The largest disk I've had in a M600 is a 9GB.

l8r/Jspr



