Depends what you call "possible".  I once loaded Saveset B of (I think)
VMS V5.3 onto an RD52 (30 MB) in an emergency and, telling the system to
use the standard system startup procedure instead of the "continue
installation" one which would proceed to complain bitterly about the
lack of disk space and refuse to proceed.  The result was usable enough
that I could proceed to patch up the un-backed-up RD53 that had just had
the first track (home block, MFD etc) scrozzled.

>I've heard of someone squeezing OpenVMS V7.2, TCPIP, and DECwindows onto a
>machine with less than 16MB RAM, but I wouldn't want to try it.

I've had 5.3 plus DECwindows and CMU TCP/IP in 5MB (and on an RD53) in a
II.  It wasn't exactly a rocket and I scrounged up an extra 4MB card
(MS630-BB) fairly quickly -- this was when uVAX IIs, although long
superseded were still considered vaguely useful, so the 8MB -CA cards
were not easily available second hand.  -BBs could be got because the
BA23 chassis has space for only two memory cards, which means to upgrade
to 16MB requires tossing out any existing cards to make room for the
-CAs or third party equivalent.  (I've got a 16 MB Clearpoint (I think) 
card somewhere in my hoarde of Qbus etc cards.)

VMS's memory requirements don't grow *that* much from release to release.
6.something introduced a disk cache, but that just gets out of the way
when memory is tight.

I still run VMS V6.2 on a 3100 with 12 MB -- it's not running X so it's
not *much* worse than my 32 MB 3600 with X.  In fact it looks pretty
happy really:

