  1280 x 1024 at 66Hz, and 1280 x 1024 at 72Hz.

:The VLC4000 is a VS48K-CA.

  There are a couple of different graphics controllers used with the
  VAXstation 4000 VLC -- knowing what the console calls the graphics
  display would be handy.  Use the alternate console to find out...

:The video output on the VLC seems identical to the 4000/60, the usual 3 wire
:RGB BNC cable.

  Again, there are a couple of different graphics controllers used with 
  the VAXstation 4000 model 60 -- knowing what the console calls the 
  graphics display would be handy.  Again, use the alternate console to 
  find out...  Most seem to share either 1280 x 1024 at 66Hz or 1280 x 
  1024 at 72Hz, with a few other resolutions mixed in...

:When I hook up the monitor to the VLC, there is a very distorted image that
:is unreadable. 

  Well, this combination is obviously wrong...

  I will assume you have already tried flipping the resolution switch 
  on the monitor (with the power off), and trying that...

:I don't have a VT3XX around to verify the hardware in the VLC.

  Most any serial terminal or PC terminal emulator can be used...
  The connector pinouts for the MMJ and PC DB9 are in the OpenVMS FAQ...

  There exists some general information on matching up video monitors
  and graphics controllers in the OpenVMS FAQ...

:While we're at it, the VLC only has one of the old-fashined ethernet
:adapters, one of those big plugs. I'm sorry I don't know the proper type
:description.  I do have RJ45 and BNC ethernet in the area.  What is the
:simplest way to get the VLC hooked up to the network?

  This is AUI connection originally intended to connect to a "ThickWire" 
  (10Base5) Ethernet transceiver such as the H4000 or H4005.  You will 
  need a transceiver such as a DESTA or DECXM, which will permit the AUI 
  transceiver cable to connect to a ThinWire (10Base2) network.  Single- 
  or multiport repeaters are typically used to interconnect twisted pair 
  (10BaseT) to ThinWire networks.

