In comp.terminals Alex van Denzel <bitbucket@adenzel.demon.nl> wrote:
> Derek Konigsberg wrote:
>  
>> ... I think it has to do with flow control.  When using no
>> flow control (or hardware flow control, which seems as good as none),
>> I get garbage characters when viewing large quantities of scrolling
>> text. (like over-sized "?" and such) 

> The over-sized "?" are IIRC signed of framing errors, or buffer
> overflows.
> 
> I don't know if the linux tty ports are actually using hardware flow
> control.

Probably depending on your choice of distribution, Linux is pretty happy
dealing with dumb terminals straight out of the box - certainly with my
Slackware installation, I only had to make one or two small adjustments
before I was up and running.

>> With "XOFF at 64" toggled on,
>> however, the problem mostly goes away (you don't see it very often,
>> except once in a while in some programs).  However, as a side-effect,
>> ^Q's pop up in programs like Emacs (and other stuff)
>
> Those ^Q's must mean, that the flow control characters ^S and ^Q aren't
> working at the linux level. The terminal sends a ^S to signal the server
> to stop sending, and also stops displaying! If the server doesn't handle
> ^S you don't see it. I guess in this case, the server doesn't handle
> ^Q's, but echos them, so you see them.
> If software flow control is implemented normally, ^S/^Q shouldn't be
> noticed by applications, not even in RAW mode (If I Recall Correctly, so
> please correct me if I'm wrong)

The problem lies with Emacs, in that it directly interprets the flow control
characters as being command characters - so rather than stopping output and
waiting for a ^Q when it receives a ^S, it just thinks that the user has
pressed ctrl-Q and blithely continues on it's merry way. The fact that the
terminal then starts to panic and churns out a couple more ^Q's doesn't
really help matters, either!

There is a fairly simple workaround which is described in the Emacs FAQ - 
refer to sections 110 and 111. I never use Emacs, so I haven't tried it.

If you want to run a VT-2/3/420 terminal without flow control, you need to
set it at 9600 baud with jump-scrolling. Anything else will just be too fast
and cause the buffer overruns you've been experiencing.

Good luck! 

Simon Coombs.

