If anyone wants to see it, it's a few clicks off www.sun.com, and has a
cute java player so anyone with a stable jvm can watch it ^^;;

That's the first thing that worried me.

Next, Compaq wasn't even _mentioned_ in their entire spiel.

This could be because VMS has had most of their wonderful new features
for years, on both VAX and Alpha platforms, and they don't want to
mention that their competetion is in a fine possition to stomp them, if
the competetion's marketing department actually started trying.

I really think Sun is one of the companies Compaq should be treating as
competetion here.

Sun is busily stomping into the large datacenter markets that VMS
traditionally occupies, and is also vigorously going after the
workstation market as well -- the 360Mhz Ultra 5 (a pitiful little beast
with 256k of external cache) is less than $2000 (barely, but still)

The DS10L is supposed to be coming out soon, with a faster proccessor
and other good stuff, but it isn't here, and it will still be in the
range of the Sun Ultra 10, which the plain-old DS10 outperforms quite
nicely.

If there was a well-marketed box with performance/numbers at least as
good as an Ultra 10 440Mhz box, and a price of under $1900 for a usable
system, I think it would do really well.

These boxen are very important -- by being cheap, powerful and plentiful
they bring in new customers, developers and younglings who spec larger
systems.

These boxen get in the door much more easily than the ones costing even
$500 more, and once in the door prove their worth, making larger box
purchases more likely.

Of course, I also think fixing the web site would help, and getting
marketing to actually push Compaq's assets, and that one should be able
to get VMS _cheap_ on small boxen for commercial use.

A lot of people paying normal prices for support/services on a $250 VMS
license is a lot better than that same lot of people paying Sun or
Microsoft for support/services on their free or hidden-cost licenses,
which is what I fear will happen if things don't change.

Ah well.
