In article <389c4003$1@news.kapsch.co.at>, eplan@kapsch.net (Peter LANGSTOEGER) writes:
:I know it is possible to remote trigger a boot of satellites via MOP.
..
:Is it possible, too ?
:I think so (at least with VAXstation 4000 and DEC3000). But see above.


  The NCP LOAD and TRIGGER commands depend on the particular network
  controller and console firmware (or host software) involved on the 
  receiving end...   Some combinations support this, and some do not...
  (And those that do often require a password of some sort, for obvious 
  reasons...)  Some network controllers have enough "brains" to have this
  capability entirely self-contained, while many will require software
  assistance -- the self-contained controllers will generally know how
  to use a signal on a local bus to trigger a system reset of some sort.)

  On the VAXstation 3100 series:

    Set the following halt/power-up mode in the console:
      >>> SET HALT 3

    Enable MOP operations:
      >>> SET MOP 1

    Enable remote triggering:
      >>> SET TRIG 1

    Set the 16 hexadecimal digit password string:
      >>> SET PSWD

  Note that a standard OpenVMS VAX shutdown involves a BRB-to-self in the 
  EXCEPTION.EXE image, rather than executing a HALT.  (This loop is used 
  because it is not known what the console HALT mode is -- the execution 
  of a HALT on a shutdown might well lead to a system reboot otherwise...)
  To avoid modifications to EXCEPTION.EXE, set the satellite to reboot
  using an image that contains a HALT instruction.  The VAX READ_ADDR.EXE 
  image happens to be a handy and readily-available example of this:

    NCP> CLEAR NODE xxx LOAD ASSIST PARAMETER
    NCP> CLEAR NODE xxx LOAD ASSIST AGENT
    NCP> SET NODE xxx LOAD FILE MOM$LOAD:READ_ADDR.SYS

  Now, shut the satellite down normally, but with an immediate reboot 
  specified.  READ_ADDR.EXE will be loaded, print out the Ethernet address, 
  and the system will halt.  Provided that the halt/power-up action is set
  to halt the system, the system will exit to >>>, waiting for a TRIGGER.

  Now -- to undo the "damage" and allow the satellite to reboot -- use
  the following commands on the boot server.

    NCP> CLEAR NODE xxx LOAD FILE
    NCP> SET NODE xxx ALL

  Also note that the TRIGGER command does not necessarily have a listener 
  handy when OpenVMS is up and running -- the MOP listener in the console
  is not operating when OpenVMS is running, thus the system should not
  respond to TRIGGER requests.  (You would need to find or write a trigger, 
  or use the console-based MOP trigger.)


 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




