Charles Gilley <gilley@bravewc.com> writes:
> In my efforts at supporting an existing site, the question has arisen about 
> DSSI drives.  As I recall, this interface is a special SCSI.  So, am I correct 
> in concluding that only a DSSI interface will talk to a DSSI device?  Has 
> anyone tried a standard SCSI interface, or will the smoke come out?
> (I don't recall if DSSI included differential).

  It's a peculiar creature all to itself. The only stuff that will talk to
DSSI is more DSSI stuff. It's conceptually similar to SCSI at the physical
layer (it branched off somewhat before SCSI-2) but beyond that it is very
different - each DSSI disk thinks it is a HSC, speaking MSCP and DUP to one
or more hosts.

  To confuse matters, some DSSI cabling uses the same connectors as SCSI
cables, but with the opposite gender (females on cables, males on equip-
ment). The unit ID plugs are also similar, and some disk drives came in
both a DSSI (RFxx) and SCSI (RZxx) variant. But they're definitely not
interchangeable.

  DEC and 3rd parties have assorted products that let you attach SCSI disks
to DSSI hosts and vice versa, but they're part of other expensive gadgets
(HSZ's, HSJ's) and if you're just looking for recent storage, consider new
SCSI disks. And if the system is old enough that it doesn't offer SCSI, you
probably want to upgrade it too.

	Terry Kennedy             http://www.tmk.com
        terry@tmk.com             Jersey City, NJ USA



