In article <38A020A9.E3296CCD@spase.nl>, Noud van Klinken <noud@spase.nl> writes:

  Follow-ups have been set to comp.os.vms, the newsgroup that most
  commonly discusses VMS, MicroVMS and OpenVMS operating system...

:Finally, after a week trying different things,
:the microvax II boots. Thanks FAQ's

  The OpenVMS FAQ is available via a link at:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/


:I have 
: - a KA630-A.V1.3 processor

  Depending on how much memory this system has, you might be able 
  to stuff a more recent OpenVMS release onto the RD53.  For some
  details on the OpenVMS hobbyist program, see the OpenVMS FAQ.

  This (and the MicroVAX 2000/VAXstation 2000 series) is the slowest 
  VAX still supported by the current OpenVMS release...

: - TK-50 tape
: - RD53a disk

  You will want to find out if you have an RQDX3 controller, and (if 
  you do) then try to locate an RD54 disk drive to replace the RD53.
  This will get you a little more storage...  KFQSA/HSD is another
  option for connecting SCSI disks, as is a third-party Q-bus to SCSI 
  adapter.  (The KZQSA is not supported for use with SCSI magnetic disks.)

: - absolutely no documentation.

  See:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com:8000/

  This is the current documentation -- you wil definitely find differences 
  between MicroVMS V4.7 (or VAX/VMS V4.7) and the current OpenVMS VAX V7.2 
  documentation...  (Being back two major releases of the operating system 
  has that effect... :-)

: - a VMS tape although "b mua0" results in ?.. FILESTRUCT or something.

  That TK50 tape is apparently not bootable.  Most are not.  The creation
  of a bootable tape is a deliberate operation, involving a tool such as 
  STABACKIT.COM.

:It happens to start VMS4.7, from the original image found on the disk.
:I even can boot interactively with >>> b/1 dua0. Then I get the prompt
:SYSBOOT>

  Which is what should happen with the B/1" command -- this is a standard
  conversational bootstrap, and it invokes SYSBOOT.  This is part of the
  sequence described in the OpenVMS FAQ section entitled "MGMT5.  I've 
  forgotten the SYSTEM password - what can I do?".

:Can anyone please tell me how to become user SYSTEM if the password
:is unknown?

  Ayup, you are almost in.  See the OpenVMS FAQ section MGMT5...

