X-Complaints-To: newsabuse@supernews.com

I have an AlphaStation 255/300 that, over the course of the past six
months, has developed the habit of mysteriously turning itself off.
Here's the particulars:

System Configuration:
256meg of memory, VMS 7.1-2, 2GB RZ28D, 4.3GN Seagate ST15230W, and
2GB IBM DCAS-32160. CD-ROM drive. System resides in a raised-floor
environment with UPS.

Symptoms:
Every ten to thirty days the system will suddenly quit. The power
switch is in the "on" position, but there is no power at all to the
system. Turning the power switch off and then on again causes a
normal system power-up and boot.

There seems to be no pattern to the time of day - it can happen as
easily at 3am as it can at noon. I've checked the error logs and
there is nothing recorded whatsoever regarding the sudden failures.

The system operates primarily as a mail and http server. There is
never anybody logged in interactively other than myself.

Conclusions:
I suspect that the problem is a flakey power supply. Before I tear
the system down and replace the old PS, I thought I'd see if anyone
has any additional opinions about my diagnosis.

Thanks,

David Spencer
PageWeavers
spencer@PageWeavers.spaamfree.com



