> .........................  Since I have the manual in hand,
> I wonder whether the Computer History museum or Ebay is the
> best destination when it comes time to dispose of my assets.

The Charles Babbage Institute strikes me as the best place to dispose
of papers.  Unlike the Computer Museum (or it's left coast offspring)
the Institute is basically run by librarians and archivists and is
devoted to preservation of the paper trail that documents the
development of computers.

Artifacts, on the other hand, should go to museums or collectors,
depending on their nature.  The Smithsonian (with whom I've split some
of my punched card collection) is the most likely to properly preserve
of genuinely historic artifacts, but the least likely to accept random
stuff.  My experience with the Smithsonian is that they work best when
given a list of what you have, from which they pick what they believe
is worth adding to their collection.

The Computer Museum and its ilk are more likely to accept stuff, but
they have such limited resources too, and when a privately funded museum
collection gets overbig for the available storage and display space,
the common outcome, whether in the art world or the technology world,
is a trip to Sothelbys, E-bay or some other route into the collector's
world.

				Doug Jones
				jones@cs.uiowa.edu

(This E-mail written on an IBM RT running AOS, aka BSD 4.3 UNIX)



