The problem of preservation concerns all types of historical items.
However, other types of museums have dedicated professionals devoted
to preserving items.  Unfortunately, computer museums haven't had
time to evolve to the point where curators have developed the
expertise to preserve the historical items.  Part of the problem may
be related to funding.  Part of the problem may be related to
legal/copyright issues (which can futher impact the funding
problem).  Part of the problem may be the speed at which computer
technology has evolved.  Part of the problem may be related to the
size of the older machines.  Thus, a lot of the preservation efforts
are carried out by amateurs.  Unfortunately, this results in quite a
few collections being in private hands.  The good side, though, is
that even though some of the collections are private, some of the
collectors have been creating electronic museums on the web.  While
this isn't as nice as being able to stand in the presence of the
(operating) historic system, at least people have a chance to view
the systems, and, if the collectors are adept enough to have the
machine operating and connected to the net, even run applications on
the historic systems.

I'm not a big fan of selling stuff on e-bay, either.  However, I
ocassionally surf it and will pick up a piece of historic equipment,
if only to keep it from going into the trash bin.  I've amassed a
bit of historic stuff, both by selected acquisitions from places
like e-bay and also by 'dumpster diving'.  Sometimes, I've been
known to rescue a piece of equipment and donate it to another
collector since it doesn't fit with my collection and I know someone
else can take better care of it (I once rescued a PDP-11/03 from
going to the dump, and donated it to a collector of PDP-11
equipment, since I mainly collect IBM equipment and the PDP-11
didn't fit with my collection, even though it was an excellent
machine.).

I know we were saddened by the loss of the Computer Museum in Boston
(I was fortunate enough to visit it before it closed.).  And, I
think that the possibility of a museum closing, with the loss of
their collection, is a concern to a lot of potential donators.  One
answer may be to permanently loan an item to a museum.  Thus, if the
museum closes, you retain ownership of the item, so it can't be
auctioned or sold by the museum.

This doesn't solve the problem of software, though.  Due to the
copyright problem, software usually can't be copied.  I know some
companies have released rights on some of their products (I've heard
that IBM has assisted with the software on a 1620 restoration
project, and I think I've heard that DEC had a reasonable policy
concerning some of their historic code.  Plus, wasn't it Caldera
that released some of the older CP/M code?).  Still, there is enough
software that hasn't been released to cause problems.  It's a shame
that there can't a group like the Project Gutenberg team for
software.

Dave

P.S.  Standard Disclaimer:  I work for them, but I don't speak for them.




