DEC, up through the late 80's, was quite good at donating "classic"
software products, tools, games, and other goodies to the DECUS
library.  DECUS trashed all their older stuff in the early 90's,
but I've been working since then to read in any DECUS tapes, 8"
floppies, DECTapes, etc., and put them on-line.  In particular, there
are several thousand megabytes of PDP-11 DECUS library
software online at

  http://metalab.unc.edu/pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11/

The DECUS PDP-10 libarary is also available online, at

  http://pdp-10.trailing-edge.com/

in the "freeware" section.

I also have a library of several hundred PDP-8 DECTapes which I hope
to organize soon.

The real problem isn't reading the old media (I've had that under
control for years), but presenting the library in a coherent
understandable form.  The classic DECUS way of organizing it - under
the DECUS library number - makes perfect sense to me, but only
because I've been dealing with it in this way for decades and I've
got printed DECUS catalogs here.

Also very worthy of mention (and I know that Dennis already knows
about it) is the PDP-11 Unix Preservation Society archive.  See

  http://minnie.cs.adfa.oz.au/PUPS/

for more information.

> Plus, wasn't it Caldera
> that released some of the older CP/M code?). 

Not "released" (they still retain copyright), but if you look
at the comp.os.cpm FAQ you'll find pointers to much of the
Digital Research stuff that's available.

On a related note, I've got source code here to some (many? most?) of
the early Microsoft products - you know, things like MBASIC, etc.,
that say at the top:

.TITLE BASIC Mpu 8080/8085/Z80/8086 (5.11)  Bill Gates/Paul Allen

INCLUDE BASIC.MAC

;SUBTTL VERSION 5.11 -- NOT MANY FEATURES TO GO

;COPYRIGHT 1975 BILL GATES AND PAUL ALLEN

;BILL GATES WROTE A LOT OF STUFF

;PAUL ALLEN WROTE OTHER STUFF AND FAST CODE

;MONTE DAVIDOFF WROTE THE MATH PACKAGE

;ORIGINALLY WRITTEN ON THE PDP-10 FROM
;FEBRUARY 9 TO APRIL 9 1975

I'd like there to be some historical archive where this Microsft
source code could be deposited, but so far I've gotten no interest
from anyone in Redmond, and nobody else seems interested either.
As you pointed out, there is a copyright problem, as well as the
fact that I could never afford a legal team like Microsoft's, so
perhaps it'd be best if I just trashed those tapes and 8" floppies
and stuck to the DECUS stuff which was explicitly in the public
domain when it was contributed.

