 Many years ago in a job far far away I was asked to evaluate a
"service" from DEC called the Consultants Reference Guide or
something like that.  It came in a set of high quality dark red IIRC
loose leaf binders and every three months or so one would recieve
an upgrade of new and replacement pages.  I told my boss that I
thought it was useless but DEC sent in one of their pretty sales
babes and my boss decided to buy it anyway and, of course, now
it was my responsibility to make room for it and keep it upgraded.
This was almost a total waste of time but for the very first document
in the first binder, a small "history book" titled Digital Equipment
Corporation Nineteen Fifty-Seven to the Present.  Some years
later the service was stopped when my management changed and
I threw out the binders, keeping the history book.  Here's what it
says about the PDP-2 and PDP-3:
Two new products, the PDP-2 and PDP-3, were proposed
while the PDP-1 was in production.  The PDP-2 never
progressed beyond the initial stages.  The PDP-3, a 36-bit
computer based on the PDP-1 concepts, was designed but not
built by the company.

I don't believe there was ever a PDP-20 so to speak unless
you consider the DECSYSTEM-20 as the "PDP-20".

BTW the copy of the "history book" I have has copywrite
dates of 1972, 1973, 1974, 1975, 1976, 1978 and the
last year of data is for 1977.  As the guide was purchased
in the early to mid 80's I'm assuming this was the last
version of this publication.  Anybody know anything
different.  It's a nice little presentation of DEC hardware
and software organized chronologically, 88 pages total.
Regards,
