In comp.sys.dec Peter Seebach <seebs@plethora.net> wrote:
> How about 4.4BSD on a VAX?  NetBSD/vax, alive and well.

  I wouldn't classify NetBSD/vax as CSRG 4.4BSD. I assume it's come a long
way and diverged from 4.4BSD. And given the state of the VAX support in
4.4, they may have approached it as a new port (possibly re-using old code)
rather than using the VAX parts.

  At one time, I had a VAX-11/785 running a pre-4.4 release which we (at
SPC) internally called "4.3-Carson City". It was based on the last 4.4
pre-release that still supported the old memory management model used on
4.3/VAX. Another developer and I were working on getting the new 4.4 kernel
code to compile and run right, while another developer was looking at the
system utilities (a lot of stuff from "ps" to "vmstat" needed hacking to
run with the "neither fish nor fowl" kernel we were using).

  Things were going along nicely, and we were getting ready to integrate
some changes sent by Paul Vixie, when we had a simultaneous failure of
6 RA81 hard disks.

  As an interim step to pacify the users (yes, this oddball system had
users on it while we were developing!), we set up an old 386DX/25 PC w/
16MB and some IDE hard disks, running a pre-beta version of BSD/OS (it
was version 0.2.2, IIRC). The users thought that the PC was much faster
than the VAX (and we agreed), so the VAX project got scrapped and SPC
switched to BSD/OS for their Unix computing.

  To keep this related to the "C-Kermit" in the subject, fdc had an
account on the Carson City VAX and was doing Kermit builds/tests on it.
I kept having to tell him to not call it 4.4BSD, since this was before
4.4 came out and I didn't want him (or me!) to be mobbed by people going
"where can I get it?!?!?!".

