We currently have several sites running mixed VAX and Alphastation
clusters. The VAXes are mostly 4000/60 boxes, and the Alphas are mostly
Alphastation 200 boxes. Each site currently has a couple of expansion
boxes hanging off the VAX boot nodes, housing several DEC disks, mostly
RZ58s I think. All boxes are running OpenVMS 7.1.

We now have a requirement for much larger storage on these systems, in
the region of 30 - 40Gb. I have done some investigations into possible
upgrade paths, mostly by searching Deja-News, and I think we have the
following options:

1. Ditch a couple of our RZ58s from the expansion boxes and replace
them with, say, IBM Ultrastar 18ES 18.2Gb Ultra SCSI (Narrow) disks.

2. Buy new pizza boxes or whatever and add the disks to these, either
from the VAXs or the Alphastations.

3. Buy a new SCSI controller for the Alphastation and then a new Ultra2
Wide SCSI 36 or 50Gb disk

I would appreciate this groups comments on any of the above, as I don't
want to make the wrong decision. Does the brand of disk matter, eg we
have been quoted for Seagate 18LX 18Gb Narrow SCSI disks, but they are
about �150 (gbp) more than the 'equivalent' IBM disks.

Thanks in advance for any help anyone can give

Gordon Maxwell
