Thomas,

Chips on the underside of the board probably don't get much airflow, and hadn't
thought about that at all. (yet another area to deal with on my UDB) One
solution may be to epoxy small pieces of thin aluminium to the offending chips
to provide heat sinking. Make the metal larger than the chip, making sure that
overhanging bits are insulated to prevent shorts. If there is any airflow at all
under the board, a small heatsink should be quite effective.

A similar idea was used on Maxtor XT2190 aka Dec RD54, where there was a serious
failure mode on early drives due to spindle motor driver transistors
overheating.

High temperature kills electronics, the cooler you can keep it all, the
better...

Rgrds, Chris 


