>
> 2. Buy new pizza boxes or whatever and add the disks to these, either
> from the VAXs or the Alphastations.
>
> 3. Buy a new SCSI controller for the Alphastation and then a new Ultra2
> Wide SCSI 36 or 50Gb disk

  I wouldn't buy any new narrow disks - either get Ultra Wide disks or
Ultra 2/LVD disks.

  The motherboard SCSI controller in the AS200 isn't any great performer -
it's a narrow SCSI implementation using an older NCR/Symbios/LSI Logic
chip.

  I'd suggest a new SCSI controller - Intraserver makes some boards with
native VMS support - see:

http://www.intraserver.com/products/alpha4000.html (dual UW SCSI + Ethernet)
http://www.intraserver.com/products/alpha6110u2.html (U2/LVD SCSI + Ethernet)
http://www.intraserver.com/products/alpha6000.html (single/dual U2/LVD SCSI)

  Be sure to get the VMS version of the card - it has an additional EPROM
on board needed to work with the DEC 3rd-party boot code.

  For folks who want a DEC solution, DEC calls the first of the above boards
the KZPCM-DA.

  One big disk vs. several smaller ones depends on whether your I/O workload
would benefit from multiple spindles, and you didn't give enough info for ad-
vice in that area.

  I like Kingston (http://www.kingston.com/storage/default.asp) for storage
enclosures.

