On Tue, 01 Feb 2000 14:27:46 -0500, Paul Roskos <paul.roskos@bms.com>
wrote:

>My company recently removed two DEC VAX computers from service, a
>6310 and 8810. The 8810 had a Fortran program that I need to
>convert to a version of Fortran that runs on a PC.
>
>The program contains several statements of the form,
>"ENCODE(1,10,File(4)) PASS(1)." Could anyone tell me what the
>"Encode" statement does?

Well, if you use Compaq Visual Fortran, you don't have to convert it -
ENCODE is supported.

ENCODE is an extension that performs a function similar to the Fortran
77 standard "internal WRITE" statement (ENCODE predates F77).  The
statement you show says write one character, using FORMAT 10, into
array element FILE(4) the formatted value of PASS(1).  FILE is
probably an INTEGER array though it could be CHARACTER or any type.
If it happened to be CHARACTER*1, a F77 equivalent might be:

	WRITE (FILE(4), 10) PASS(1)

See
http://www.digital.com/fortran/docs/lrm/lrm0632.htm#encode_decode_stmts
for more information on ENCODE/DECODE.


Send Visual Fortran support requests to vf-support@compaq.com

