In comp.sys.dec Dennis Ritchie <dmr@bell-labs.com> wrote:

Hello Dennis!

> .........................  Since I have the manual in hand,
> I wonder whether the Computer History museum or Ebay is the
> best destination when it comes time to dispose of my assets.

> 	Dennis

If I might be so bold to suggest, after working around things
historical going back electronic for 100 years...., IMHO, anything
other than Ebay would be the way to go.  We all do generally accept
the notion that you are the Godfather of this thing UNIX, and I would
really think that anything you feel important enough to be worth saving
or noting historically ought to go into some sort of real museum type
setting (not necessarily someone's personal collection kind of thing),
for safekeeping, and definitely not cast to the winds on Ebay.  My
gut feeling is that such documentation, notes, items, whatever, will
be more useful to the historical end of the UNIX community down the
road, if kept in some sort of real museum setting.  The things in
the PUPS archives, and online in your web site, are good for many
kinds of things electronic.  Yet, the hardcopy things still need
to be considered and preserved, historically, too.  Sadly, I have
seen too many historically important artifacts relating to the
earlier generation of electronics (telegraphy, radio, etc.), become
lost in time, forever, when they go into private collections or
out to the highest bidder.  Important things become too easily lost.
In the ``modern computer era'' (gee it has only been about 40 years),
with the hustle and bustle and mad rush to the next quantum leap
forward, the history has not been adequately preserved.  We just have
not thought ahead of time about such things, with the rabid pace of
technology.  That will bite us down the road, methinks.   So, FWIIW,
my zwei pfennigs would be to not Ebay things, but try to gather them
up into some meaningful group, for that time that we all will face,
when we retire, or take a long journey elsewhere.  If possible,
multiple copies of anything important should be archived so they
don't become lost in the cracks of time.

Just thinking out loud, but with sincere honesty, and great appreciation
for what you, Ken, and all the others have done.....

Bob






