You need a monitor that can do sync on green.  The three wires from the
Alpha are the red, green and blue (RGB) signals, with the sync on the
green.  VGA/SVGA use RGB, horizontal sync and vertical sync.  In
addition to the sync on green the monitor must support high sync
frequencies.  The horizontal sync on most of the DEC monitors run from
60 to 75 Hz.

You might find a converter box that will give you the horizontal and
vertical sync signals, but, if the monitor won't do sync on green it
most likely won't support the high sync rates.

You need to find a SVGA monitor that supports sync on green.  Some of
the new models do that.  I have an HP at home that works, but I don't
recall the model number.  Something like Axxxxx.  I have heard from
others that some of the high end NEC's will work.  If you know which DEC
monitor works with your system you can look at www.monitorworld.com and
find the horizontal and vertical sync frequencies.  When you get a SVGA
monitor make sure it can handle those frequencies.  



