:In article <20000130014459.29824.00000452@ng-ck1.aol.com>, choaglin@aol.com (CHoaglin) writes:
:>While cleaning some stuff out of my storage space, I came across a
:>DECTalk...It's a small unit, in a case similar to a DEREP or a VT240. 

  This is likely a DECtalk-I DTC01 series voice synthesizer.

:>I remember reading somewhere that it's a speech synthesizer of some kind, 
:>in which case I would like to use it to produce speech effects in electronic 
:>music compositions. Unfortunately, I can't seem to find much of anything 
:>about it on the web...If anybody's got a manual for one, or has some 
:>information about the DECTalk, I'd be very interested in hearing from them...

  At its simplest, it can be connected on a serial line from the host
  system, and treated like a printer.  Various folks then connected a 
  terminal to the DECtalk.  The DECtalk itself is controlled by a
  variety of ANSI escape sequences.

:Check the CONOLD CDs. In the "OpenVMS Operating System Archived Manuals"
:chapter there is the DECtalk RTL (DTK$ functions) manual.

  That particular RTL might provide you with some general information,
  but the API is obviously exclusively for the use of OpenVMS.  The
  DECtalk itself operates much like a serial ASCII printer, and is
  *not* dependent on any particular host system.  

  The source code to the OpenVMS VAX DECtalk API is available on the 
  OpenVMS Freeware, in:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/freeware/DECTALK/...

  Manuals are a little difficult to come by, there are discussions around
  digging up any old DECtalk-I (DTC01) and DECtalk-III (DTC03) information
  that might still be around for inclusion on the next next OpenVMS 
  Freeware, but there have been no decisions made as yet...

